      "followup": {
        "prompt": "Explain what this distance measures and the limits of this search without changing the scene.",
        "reply": "The 8,008 km is the straight-line distance between the centre of Mars and the centre of Psyche, taken from the loaded SPICE kernels. It is not altitude above the Martian surface. Mars's radius is about 3,390 km. That would put the spacecraft roughly 4,600 km above the surface, but that figure is my own rough subtraction, not something the search reported.\n\nThe search has these limits:\n- **Window:** It covered only 2023-10-13 to 2031-11-01, the span the loaded kernels cover. It says nothing about times outside that.\n- **Time step:** It sampled every hour. That step limits how complete the search is, so a very brief minimum could be missed or its exact time and range slightly off.\n- **No correction:** I used no light-time or aberration correction. These are geometric distances, not what an observer would see.\n- **Local minima:** It listed local minima, and only two turned up. The 2026 one is the smaller, so it is the closest within this window.\n- **Source:** The distances are only as good as the 26 loaded kernels. The scene's rendering is for illustration.\n- **Framing:** The framing check confirms Mars and Psyche are inside the view. It does not prove they are unobstructed.\n\nI haven't changed anything just now.",
